## 6. Functional requirements

Requirement IDs are stable: `FR-<area>-<n>`. **Must** is mandatory. **Should** is expected unless there is a stated reason not to.

### 6.1 Configuration and topology (CFG)

| ID | Requirement |
|---|---|
| FR-CFG-1 | The system **must** be configured declaratively with a set of apps. Each app has a suite path, optional default networks (default: both), optional app headers, one or more envs, and optional env comparisons. |
| FR-CFG-2 | Each env **must** support: a host map (canonical → env host), headers, a network override, a run schedule (starts every target of the env), and a comparison schedule (starts the env's network comparison). |
| FR-CFG-3 | Targets **must** be derived as every app × env × allowed network. No target is listed by hand. |
| FR-CFG-4 | A **network comparison** **must** be generated automatically for every env that has both networks. |
| FR-CFG-5 | An **env comparison** **must** be created for each `compare_envs` entry (candidate env, baseline env, network, defaulting to prod, plus an optional schedule). |
| FR-CFG-6 | Configuration **must** be validated before deploy, with these rules: name patterns; reserved names; runner-name length ≤ 21; no runner-name collisions; networks a non-empty subset of {prod, staging}; every env comparison references two different existing envs that both have the network; no duplicate env comparisons. Each failure **must** produce an actionable message, e.g. "use a shorter app or env name". |
| FR-CFG-7 | Headers **must** merge in the order global < app < env, with the more specific winning. Header values **may** reference secrets as `{{KEY}}`. |
| FR-CFG-8 | The resolved topology (apps, targets, comparisons, header **names** only, host maps, legacy aliases) **must** be published as one versioned document. Its version is a content hash. Services **must** pick up a new version without redeploying code. Header values and secrets **must not** be in the topology. |
| FR-CFG-9 | Adding an app **must** need only a suite file and a config entry. Adding an env **must** need only one config entry. |
| FR-CFG-10 | Global settings **must** cover: retention days for results and for audit, the primary and verification AI models, the pairing window, comparison concurrency, screenshot concurrency, HTTP concurrency, and screenshot settle time. |

### 6.2 Test suites (SUITE)

| ID | Requirement |
|---|---|
| FR-SUITE-1 | Each app **must** have one suite in CSV format with these columns: `id`, `name`, `url`, `method`, `body`, `request_headers`, `expected_status`, `expected_headers`, `body_contains`, `run_on`, `screenshot`, `screenshot_delay`, `screenshot_wait_for`, `enabled`. Header names are case- and punctuation-insensitive. Multi-line cells **must** be supported. |
| FR-SUITE-2 | Suites **must** be stored centrally and read by the runner at the start of every run. Editing a suite **must not** require redeploying runners. |
| FR-SUITE-3 | Every run **must** record which suite it used: key, SHA-256 and row count. |
| FR-SUITE-4 | A row with no `id` **must** get a stable fallback id derived from its row number. Rows with `enabled = no` (or false/n/0/off) or with no URL **must** be skipped. |
| FR-SUITE-5 | **Assertions:**<br>• `expected_status`: one or more allowed codes.<br>• `expected_headers`, one per line: `Name` (must exist), `!Name` (must be absent), `Name: text` (contains), `Name: /regex/flags`.<br>• `body_contains`, one per line: text or `/regex/`, with a `!` prefix meaning "must not contain". |
| FR-SUITE-6 | **Scopes.** `run_on` and any `[scope …]` line prefix in `request_headers`, `expected_headers`, `body_contains` and the screenshot columns **must** limit a row or line to matching targets, using these scope words:<br>• `all`, `both` or blank: every target.<br>• bare `prod` / `staging`: always the **network**, even if an env has that name.<br>• `net:<n>`: the network.<br>• `env:<e>`: the env.<br>• `<env>.<network>` or a full target id: one target.<br>• any other bare word: the env.<br>Several scope words **may** be listed. |
| FR-SUITE-7 | `{{KEY}}` placeholders in URL, body and headers **must** be resolved from the secret store at run time. An unresolved placeholder **must** be left as is. |
| FR-SUITE-8 | `screenshot = no` **must** skip the screenshot. Only GET rows get screenshots. `screenshot_delay` (seconds, capped at 30) and `screenshot_wait_for` (a CSS selector, waited for up to 15 s) **must** tune that row's capture. |
| FR-SUITE-9 | The system **should** be able to pull a suite from a published spreadsheet CSV URL per app (`sync`), as long as the sheet is laid out in the suite's column format. |

### 6.3 Test execution (EXEC)

| ID | Requirement |
|---|---|
| FR-EXEC-1 | Each target **must** have its own runner that executes the app's enabled, in-scope suite rows against the env's hosts. Each URL's canonical host is rewritten through the env's host map. |
| FR-EXEC-2 | **Prod network:** normal DNS resolution. |
| FR-EXEC-3 | **Staging network:** for each hostname, the runner **must** follow the CNAME chain to its Akamai edge hostname (`*.edgekey.net`, `*.edgesuite.net`, `*.akamaized.net`), resolve the `-staging` equivalent, and pin the connection to that IP. SNI and the Host header stay unchanged. Hosts not on Akamai **must** fall back to normal DNS, and the result **must** carry a note saying so. |
| FR-EXEC-4 | Every request **must** send the Akamai `Pragma` debug headers and an identifiable user agent. The runner **must** capture at least: `location`, `cache-control`, `content-type`, `x-cache`, `x-cache-remote`, `x-check-cacheable`, `x-cache-key`, `x-akamai-request-id`, `x-akamai-staging`, `x-sfdc-page-render-type`, `x-sfdc-content-source`, `x-sfdc-agent-cohort-decision`, `x-sfdc-agent-cohort-source`. |
| FR-EXEC-5 | **Redirects:**<br>• Followed up to 10 hops, carrying cookies set along the way. 301/302/303 switch to GET; 307/308 keep the method and body.<br>• If the row expects only 3xx, the first real redirect is asserted instead, so redirect rules can be checked with `Location:`.<br>• Same-path hops that only change the query (e.g. a geo `?bc=` redirect) are always followed.<br>• Every hop **must** be recorded in a redirect chain: method, URL, status, start offset, duration, edge IP, cache header, location. |
| FR-EXEC-6 | Each request **must** time out after 15 s, with a recorded error. |
| FR-EXEC-7 | HTTP checks **must** run with configurable concurrency (default 10). |
| FR-EXEC-8 | **Screenshots** **must** be taken in a real browser that uses the same staging pinning, headers, user agent and viewport (1366×900) as the HTTP check.<br>• Target-specific headers go only to the suite's own hostnames, never to third parties.<br>• Analytics and ad beacons and media are blocked. Chat and experiment scripts are kept, because they change the page. |
| FR-EXEC-9 | **Screenshot readiness.** Capture in this order:<br>1. Wait for the DOM, then for the network to settle.<br>2. Wait up to 15 s until the viewport looks loaded: fonts loaded, visible images decoded, and no visible skeleton, shimmer, spinner, loading or `aria-busy` element, including inside open shadow roots.<br>3. Wait the configured settle time plus the row's delay.<br>4. Freeze CSS animations, transitions, the caret and videos, then capture a JPEG.<br>A capture that still looked unready **must** be flagged `still loading`. |
| FR-EXEC-10 | Screenshots **must** be taken in parallel by a separate fan-out worker (default 20 at a time per run). Each starts as soon as its check is done. A setting **must** allow falling back to in-runner capture. A crashed browser tab or browser **must** be replaced or relaunched, without losing the test results. |
| FR-EXEC-11 | **Time budget.** A run **must** stop starting screenshots 60 s before its budget (30 s in-runner) and stop starting tests 15 s before it. Default budget: 780 s, under the 14-minute runner limit. Skipped tests and screenshots **must** be reported with a reason, never silently dropped. |
| FR-EXEC-12 | **Live progress.** While running, the runner **must** publish a progress document with: target, suite, start time, total, done, failed, current activity (`checks n/N`, `screenshots n/M`), the plan (ids, names, URLs), per-test results as they land, and the request metadata (by, label, request id, pin, re-run). A finished run **must** mark it finished. |
| FR-EXEC-13 | **Run result.** At the end, the runner **must** write an immutable result document: target, app, env, network, suite, start and finish times, totals, subset, request metadata, and the per-test results in sheet order. Each result has: id, name, URL, pass, errors, expectations, status, timing, captured headers, redirect chain, staging IP or edge, note, screenshot key, screenshot error, loading flag and duration. |
| FR-EXEC-14 | The runner **must** maintain a per-target index of the last 30 run summaries, newest first. The index is best effort and **must** be rebuildable from the results. |
| FR-EXEC-15 | A run with failing tests **must** be reported to the scheduler as failed, so alarms can fire, and **must** be distinguishable from a crash. |
| FR-EXEC-16 | **Run requests.** A run **may** be parameterised by a one-time request: a subset of ids, by, label, pinned baseline, re-run target. The runner consumes it once. A request older than 15 minutes **must** be ignored. A failure to load the suite **must not** consume the request. |

### 6.4 Run control (RUN)

| ID | Requirement |
|---|---|
| FR-RUN-1 | Operators **must** be able to start: one target; every target of an env; or both targets of a configured comparison. |
| FR-RUN-2 | A start **may** carry a **subset** of test ids (≤ 500), a **change label** (≤ 120 chars, e.g. `CHG-1234`), and, for comparisons, a **pinned baseline run**. With a pinned baseline, only the candidate runs. |
| FR-RUN-3 | A pinned baseline **must** be a run of that comparison's baseline target and must still exist. An expired pin **must** be rejected with an explanation. |
| FR-RUN-4 | Runs **must** be credited to the signed-in user. Only the owner may set "by" freely. |
| FR-RUN-5 | A **full** run, or a **scheduled** run, that finds its target busy **must** be skipped and reported as "already running". It must not be queued. |
| FR-RUN-6 | **Queue.** A subset, pinned or re-run request that finds a target busy **must** be queued per app instead of dropped:<br>• Entries with the same targets, pin and re-run **merge**: their test ids are added up, and a full run covers any subset.<br>• A comparison's entry starts **both** sides together, so the two runs pair.<br>• The queue is first-in first-out per target. An older entry holds its targets.<br>• At most 50 entries per app. Entries older than 6 h are dropped.<br>• The queue **must** drain when a runner changes state, and periodically as a fallback.<br>• A start that loses a race is re-queued at the front. |
| FR-RUN-7 | Operators **must** be able to list queued entries (position, targets, ids, by, label, pinned, re-run of) and remove one. |
| FR-RUN-8 | Operators **must** be able to stop one target, or every running target of an env. |
| FR-RUN-9 | **Schedules.** Env run schedules and comparison schedules **must** start runs with `by = schedule`. |
| FR-RUN-10 | **Run state** of each target **must** be one of: ready, starting, running (with progress), stopping/updating, or crashed. A crash is a runner failure that is not a test failure, recorded with its time and reason, and shown only if it is newer than the last good result. A progress document unfinished for more than 15 min **must** be treated as a dead run. |
| FR-RUN-11 | A start response **must** report per target whether it started, was queued (with queue id, position and what it waits for), or was refused (with the reason). |

### 6.5 Comparisons (CMP)

#### 6.5.1 Pairing
| ID | Requirement |
|---|---|
| FR-CMP-1 | When a run's result lands, every configured comparison that has its target as baseline or candidate **must** be evaluated. |
| FR-CMP-2 | If the candidate run carries a pin for that comparison, it **must** be compared with the pinned baseline. |
| FR-CMP-3 | Otherwise:<br>• If the other side is still running, the comparison **must** wait for that run and record a status of "waiting".<br>• If it isn't, pair with the other side's latest run, but only if both runs started within the pairing window (default 30 min).<br>• Otherwise record "skipped" with the reason (e.g. "started 47 min apart; use Compare now"). |
| FR-CMP-4 | Each pair **must** be compared exactly once, even when the results event is delivered twice. |
| FR-CMP-5 | The latest pairing status (waiting or skipped, the reason, the run keys) **must** be readable per comparison until a newer comparison starts. |
| FR-CMP-6 | Subset runs **must** be compared only on the tests both sides ran. If there are none in common, record "skipped". |

#### 6.5.2 Functional diff (deterministic)
| ID | Requirement |
|---|---|
| FR-CMP-10 | Per test, the system **must** flag a functional difference for: a different HTTP status; a different value of `location`, `content-type`, `cache-control`, `x-check-cacheable` or `x-sfdc-content-source`; a different pass/fail; or a test that only ran on one side. |
| FR-CMP-11 | **Normalization.** `cache-control` `max-age=<n>` with n > 0 **must** compare as `max-age=N`, so cacheable is compared against non-cacheable, not the remaining TTL. `content-type` **must** compare case-insensitively. |
| FR-CMP-12 | **Host-aware.** `location` **must** be compared after mapping each side's env hosts back to the canonical hosts. When values match only after mapping, that **must** be recorded, with both raw values. |

#### 6.5.3 Visual judge
| ID | Requirement |
|---|---|
| FR-CMP-20 | When both sides have a screenshot, the pair **must** get a visual verdict: `same`, `minor`, `different` or `error`, plus a one-sentence summary and a list of differences, each with a severity (minor or major). |
| FR-CMP-21 | **Cost gate.**<br>• Byte-identical screenshots: `same` ("pixel-identical"), with no AI call.<br>• Fewer than 0.1% of pixels visibly differing: `same` ("visually identical"), with no AI call. |
| FR-CMP-22 | **Region hint.** When at most 4 changed regions are found (on a 12×12 tile grid), the judge **must** be told exactly where the pixels differ, and that everything else is identical. |
| FR-CMP-23 | The judge's instructions **must** depend on the kind:<br>• **network**: expect identical pages.<br>• **env / ad hoc**: content may legitimately differ; only broken-looking pages are `different`, and content changes are `minor`. |
| FR-CMP-24 | The judge **must** ignore: rotating carousels and banners; ads; dates; personalised content; cookie or consent banners; chat pop-ups; animation frames; per-request ids on error pages; sub-pixel noise; swapped photos or logos in an unchanged layout. A loading skeleton on one side is at most `minor`. |
| FR-CMP-25 | Listed "differences" that say the two sides are the same **must** be dropped, and the verdict **must** follow what remains. |
| FR-CMP-26 | **Second opinion.** A `different` verdict from the primary model **must** be re-checked by a stronger verification model (configurable, can be disabled). The verification model's answer is kept and marked as checked, with the first opinion attached. A verification error keeps the first verdict and records the error. |
| FR-CMP-27 | **Retries.** Model or tool-call failures **must** be retried up to 3 times with varied sampling. A persistent failure gives `error`, with the reason. One test's error **must not** stop the others. |
| FR-CMP-28 | **Known differences.** The comparison's enabled accepted visual rules **must** be matched against the found differences in a separate text-only step. Each difference is tagged with the rule it matches, if any. |
| FR-CMP-29 | Visual judging **must** run with configurable parallelism (default 10). |

#### 6.5.4 Item and comparison verdicts
| ID | Requirement |
|---|---|
| FR-CMP-30 | **Item verdict, network kind:** `different` if there is any functional diff or the visual verdict is `different`. Otherwise the visual verdict. |
| FR-CMP-31 | **Item verdict, env and ad-hoc kinds:** status, pass/fail and one-sided diffs, or a visual `different`, give `different`. Header-only diffs give at least `minor` and are flagged "to review". |
| FR-CMP-32 | Items **must** be ordered worst first: different, error, minor, same. The comparison **must** carry counts per verdict, a rollup (functional, critical, visual, minor, error, failing-on-candidate), the suite hashes of both sides with a "suites differ" flag, the model used, and progress (done/total) while running. |
| FR-CMP-33 | **Go/No-Go** **must** be computed by the rules in §7.1, only from a **full** finished comparison, after applying accepted rules. Subset comparisons are **PARTIAL** and never set the verdict. Running ones are **COMPARING**. Ad hoc has no verdict. |
| FR-CMP-34 | The newest full comparison per configured comparison **must** be its current verdict. A newer partial comparison **must** be reported alongside it, without replacing it. |
| FR-CMP-35 | The system **must** warn when the two runs of a non-pinned comparison started more than 20 min apart, because content changes in between show up as differences. |

#### 6.5.5 Re-runs, re-judges and versions
| ID | Requirement |
|---|---|
| FR-CMP-40 | **Re-run tests of a comparison.** Operators **must** be able to re-run chosen tests of a comparison, either on both sides or on the candidate only against that comparison's baseline run. The new results **must** replace those tests in a **new version** of the comparison, marked "re-run" with who, when and label. The verdict is recomputed. No new comparison is created. |
| FR-CMP-41 | Shortcuts **must** exist for: re-run every changed test of a comparison; re-run one test. |
| FR-CMP-42 | **Re-judge.** Operators **must** be able to re-run the visual judge, without re-running the tests, on: (a) items whose verdict was `error`; (b) with the visual option, also `minor` and `different` items, so newly accepted visual rules apply; (c) explicit test ids, whatever their verdict. The output is a new version, named up front so the caller can follow it, with pending ids and progress. |
| FR-CMP-43 | Comparison history **must** list each comparison once, at its newest version, with its version count and a link to the original. |
| FR-CMP-44 | Finished run and comparison documents **must** be immutable. Changes always produce new versions or are applied when the document is read (rules). |

#### 6.5.6 Ad-hoc comparisons
| ID | Requirement |
|---|---|
| FR-CMP-50 | Operators **must** be able to compare any two finished runs of the same app, optionally limited to some test ids and labelled. |
| FR-CMP-51 | **Guards:**<br>• both keys are valid run keys of the **same app** (else 400 "different apps");<br>• the keys differ;<br>• both runs exist (else 404, "expired");<br>• both are finished (else 409);<br>• they have tests in common (≤ 500);<br>• at most 2 ad-hoc comparisons running per user (else 429). |
| FR-CMP-52 | The same pair compared again **must** return the existing comparison, unless `force` is set. |
| FR-CMP-53 | **Kind derivation:** same env on different networks gives `network`, for the judge prompt; anything else gives `adhoc`. |
| FR-CMP-54 | **Presets** for a target's newest full run:<br>• **previous**: its previous full run (drift).<br>• **last_go**: the baseline run of the newest GO comparison that has this target as candidate.<br>• **prod**: the run of `<app>.prod.<network>` closest in start time. |
| FR-CMP-55 | An ad-hoc comparison **must** apply, read-only, the rules of the configured comparison with the same two targets, if one exists. No rules can be created on ad-hoc comparisons. |

### 6.6 Accepted differences (RULE)

| ID | Requirement |
|---|---|
| FR-RULE-1 | Operators **must** be able to accept a **kind** of difference for a comparison, either on every test of it or on one test. Kinds:<br>• a functional header name (e.g. `x-check-cacheable`), `status`, `assertions`, `only ran on`;<br>• an assertion error, by exact message (`error: <message>`);<br>• a visual difference (`visual: <description>`, at least 8 meaningful characters). |
| FR-RULE-2 | Rules **must** match the kind of difference, not its values. Accepting `x-check-cacheable` covers both `NO vs YES` and `YES vs NO`. |
| FR-RULE-3 | An assertion error that starts with `not run` **must not** be acceptable. |
| FR-RULE-4 | While a rule is enabled, matching differences **must** be excluded from the item verdict, counts, rollup and Go/No-Go, for every past and future comparison it applies to. They **must** stay visible and be marked as accepted, with the rule id. |
| FR-RULE-5 | A result whose assertion errors are **all** accepted **must** count as passed, marked "pass (accepted)". |
| FR-RULE-6 | **Visual rules.**<br>• Accepted differences are removed, and the verdict becomes the worst remaining severity, or `same` when none remain. The judge's original verdict is kept.<br>• Docs judged before the rule existed match by normalized text.<br>• Later comparisons match by the judge's tag (FR-CMP-28), so a reworded description of the same element still matches.<br>• When every difference is accepted, that state **must** be reported as "all N differences accepted (judged different)". |
| FR-RULE-7 | Several rules **must** be creatable in one atomic request (≤ 50), e.g. accepting every difference of one test. |
| FR-RULE-8 | Rules **must** be toggleable (enable or disable) and deletable. Re-accepting an existing (comparison, kind, test) **must** re-enable it, not create a duplicate. |
| FR-RULE-9 | Rules **must** be stored per app, with: id, comparison, kind, test, enabled, example, by, at. Concurrent edits **must not** overwrite each other (conditional write with retry). Each change increments the app's rules version. |
| FR-RULE-10 | Rules **must** be applied when a comparison is read, never written into comparison documents. Turning a rule off restores the original verdicts. Cached responses **must** be keyed to the rules version, so a document is never served under stale rules. |
| FR-RULE-11 | Rules and suites **must not** expire. |

### 6.7 Status, history and insight (INS)

| ID | Requirement |
|---|---|
| FR-INS-1 | **Portfolio.** Per app: envs; per-target state (running, failing, ok or none) with last failed/total, last run time and crash flag; the verdict of each comparison; the worst verdict (order NO-GO > REVIEW > COMPARING > GO > PARTIAL > NONE); running count; last activity. Optionally, counts of runs and comparisons newer than a given time ("new since"). |
| FR-INS-2 | **App overview.** For one app: every target with its state, live progress (done/total, failed, current phase), the last 20 runs, crash info and queued count; every comparison with its latest and latest-full summary and its pairing status; the queue; and the app's accepted rules with their version. |
| FR-INS-3 | **Run history** per target, paginated (25), newest first. Each entry has: start and finish, passed/failed/total, subset, by, label. A trend of the failed share over full runs **must** be derivable. |
| FR-INS-4 | **Run detail.** Every result of a run, with time-limited access to its screenshot. Totals, pass rate, duration and slowest test **must** be derivable. Results **must** be filterable by status, group (id prefix), host and text. |
| FR-INS-5 | **Live run.** The in-flight run's per-test results as they land, or, when idle, the runner state, latest run key and last crash. |
| FR-INS-6 | **Test history.** One test of an app across the last 20 runs of every target. Each target's history **must** be classified per §7.2 as stable, flaky, regressed, failing in all, or n/N failed. |
| FR-INS-7 | **What changed.** For a failing test, the system **must** diff the latest failing result against the last passing one: status, every captured header, redirects. Volatile fields (request id, `x-cache`, `x-cache-remote`) are excluded, and `cache-control` is marked "varies anyway". |
| FR-INS-8 | **Matrix.** Every test × target of an app, optionally one env, over the last n runs (1–20, default 10): per-cell pass/fail history and the latest errors (max 2). Sortable by failing-now, most-flaky or id. All-green rows foldable. |
| FR-INS-9 | **Needs attention.** Per app, the system **must** surface: tests that regressed, tests that fail everywhere, and tests that flip in the last 6 runs. Each entry carries a re-run action. A test failing on several targets **must** be one entry listing those targets. |
| FR-INS-10 | **Failure signatures.** Errors **must** be grouped by a normalized signature: URLs reduced to host + path, quoted values to `"…"`, numbers to `N`, at most 120 chars. Grouping applies to failed results within a run, to needs attention, and to the matrix. A signature that covers more than 50% of a run **must** be flagged **"looks environmental"** (e.g. WAF block, host down, expired secret) and ranked first. |
| FR-INS-11 | **Comparison summary.** The system **must** produce a markdown summary of a comparison for change tickets. It contains: the verdict, label, the two runs with their times, pinned flag or time gap, the rollup line, up to 12 worst items with their first diff or visual summary, a count of the rest, and a credential-free reference to the report. |
| FR-INS-12 | **Reproduce a check.** For any result, the system **must** produce an equivalent `curl` command. It includes: `--resolve` pinning to the recorded staging edge IP; the Pragma and user-agent headers; the target's header names, with secret values as `{{PLACEHOLDERS}}`; the in-scope request headers; and the recorded redirect chain and notes as comments. Secrets **must never** appear in it. |
| FR-INS-13 | **Recent activity.** A timeline of recent runs and comparisons per app, and the set of everything in flight or just finished across apps (runs, comparisons, re-judges, queued entries). |
| FR-INS-14 | **Completion events.** A user who starts a run, comparison or re-judge **must** be able to follow it to completion and be told when it finishes. A re-judge or re-run **must** expose the key of the new version as soon as it starts. |
| FR-INS-15 | **Addressability.** Every app, target, run, comparison, test and matrix **must** have a stable reference that contains no credentials. Opening it requires sign-in and then resolves to that entity. |
| FR-INS-16 | **Lateral navigation.** Given a test, run or comparison, the system **must** be able to resolve its counterpart in another env or network of the same app: the same test; the run closest in start time on the other network; the same kind of comparison for the other env. |
| FR-INS-17 | **Personal lists.** Users **should** be able to star targets and comparisons. Without stars, the most visited are used. |

### 6.8 Identity and sessions (AUTH)

| ID | Requirement |
